// games/sonic4/test/poison/poison_scene_grid.emp — POISON (see README).
//
// TARGETS: engine/level/scene_dsl.emp's `scene_forces_per_line()` ARM 5 — "any layer
// boundary not on the 8-px cell grid forces the per-line HScroll pipeline".
// EXPECTED FRAGMENT: "forced the per-line pipeline"
//
// ---- THIS POISON CHANGED SUBJECT IN P3 TASK 7, AND THE OLD SUBJECT NO LONGER EXISTS ----
//
// It used to target `layer()`'s `world_y % 8 == 0` ensure: P1's record stored a Plane-B
// CELL ROW, so an off-grid top truncated under `wy >> 3` and the constructor had to
// refuse it. World-Y re-glue widened the record's top to a PLANE LINE (`band_top_plane`),
// which makes an off-grid top REPRESENTABLE, so that ensure was removed — deliberately,
// and not into nothing. Its job moved to the forcer set: an off-grid top is legal and
// forces the per-line pipeline, because the per-cell filler can only place a cell edge
// and would silently re-quantise exactly the thing the wider field exists to express.
//
// So the guard under test is no longer "does the build refuse this?" but "does the
// derivation NOTICE this?", and a lane whose only verb is `[Error]` has to ask that
// question by asserting the FALSE half. Hence the inversion below, which is why the
// failing message is written to read correctly AT FAILURE: the diagnostic the lane
// captures is the PROOF that arm 5 fired, and a clean build here is the regression.
//
// ---- IT IS A TWO-FIXTURE DIFFERENTIAL, AND THE PASSING HALF IS LOAD-BEARING ----
//
// Fixture ON_GRID is byte-for-byte fixture OFF_GRID except for the one top (8 vs 4), and
// its ensure MUST STAY GREEN. Without it the failing half proves only that
// `scene_forces_per_line` returned nonzero for SOME reason — a forcer arm that had begun
// firing on every scene would look identical. Expected `[Error]` count is therefore 1: a
// count of 2 means the on-grid control started forcing too (arm 5 is no longer about the
// grid), and a count of 0 means arm 5 went dead and off-grid tops are being handed to the
// per-cell filler.
//
// ---- WHY BOTH FIXTURES ARE `v_factor: 15` ----
//
// Arm 5 asks its question of the PLANE IMAGE of the top, not of the authored act Y
// (scene_plane_line() is the mapping). A locked plane maps identically, so `world_y: 4`
// is plane line 4 and the fixture tests the grid rather than testing the mapping. An
// unlocked fixture would fold `4` through `>> v_factor` and land on plane line 0 — on the
// grid — and the poison would go quiet for a reason that has nothing to do with arm 5.
//
// THE ELABORATION HOOK. `scene()` and `scene_forces_per_line()` are comptime fns and are
// only elaborated when CALLED, so the two ensures below are what run the guard.
//
// THE GLOB IMPORTS ARE MANDATORY AND ARE THE AUTHOR'S OWN SPELLING. `engine.level.scene_dsl`
// is NOT a member of sigil's COMPTIME_HELPERS set (crates/sigil-harness/src/native.rs), so
// unlike `raster_dsl` nothing it defines is glob-injected into a module by the build. Every
// scene author writes this exact pair of globs (see games/sonic4/data/effects/ojz_scenes.emp),
// and a selective `use ...{layer}` is silent on the constant axis — so the glob is what
// makes this poison model an author's module rather than a special case.
module games.sonic4.test.poison.poison_scene_grid

use engine.level.scene_dsl.*
use engine.level.parallax_dsl.*

// FIXTURE OFF_GRID — one layer whose plane image (4) is off the 8-px cell grid.
const POISON_GRID_SCENE_OFF: Scene = scene(
    layers: [ layer(world_y: 4, fa: FACTOR_1, fb: FACTOR_1_2, dsa: 15, dsb: 15),
              no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer(),
              no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer() ],
    count: 1, v_factor: 15)

// FIXTURE ON_GRID — byte-for-byte fixture OFF_GRID except for the top.
const POISON_GRID_SCENE_ON: Scene = scene(
    layers: [ layer(world_y: 8, fa: FACTOR_1, fb: FACTOR_1_2, dsa: 15, dsb: 15),
              no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer(),
              no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer() ],
    count: 1, v_factor: 15)

const POISON_GRID_FORCED_OFF = scene_forces_per_line(POISON_GRID_SCENE_OFF)
const POISON_GRID_FORCED_ON  = scene_forces_per_line(POISON_GRID_SCENE_ON)

// ---- THE PASSING HALF (must stay green) ----
ensure(POISON_GRID_FORCED_ON == 0,
       "poison_scene_grid: the ON-GRID control forced per-line ({POISON_GRID_FORCED_ON}) — some other forcer arm is firing on a scene with no deform table, no anchor and PRECISION_CELL, so the failing half below proves nothing about the 8-px grid")

// ---- THE FAILING HALF — THIS DIAGNOSTIC IS THE PROOF, NOT THE PROBLEM ----
ensure(POISON_GRID_FORCED_OFF == 0,
       "poison_scene_grid: a layer top off the 8-px cell grid (world_y 4, plane line 4) forced the per-line pipeline, which is arm 5 of scene_forces_per_line doing its job. THE LANE REQUIRES THIS DIAGNOSTIC. A clean build here is the regression: it would mean arm 5 went dead and an off-grid top was being handed to Parallax_Fill_PerCell, which can only place a cell edge and would quantise it back to where P1's removed grid ensure used to refuse it")
